From 924e4eb87193dd26a72f7b8ccaec1d21fffdb4ee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 14:33:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Checker: the rare columns in base pages, read only where
 written (#628)

A reading in source order's token columns of rare columns are written
at few tokens but read at most: the expressions' classes load the
specifier finishes and the builtins at every token, and each
declaration reads its first token's flags, storage, bases and jobs. In
huge pages the writes, spread over the columns, took 20 MB at the
reader's end, the compile's peak once the scratch is given back. In
base pages the reads would map the zero page at every page they reach,
5,985 of sqlite3.c's 6,673, which a populate made 2 M kernel cycles
dearer than the huge pages.

The reader now keeps a bit per 64 bytes of the columns
(CheckInputReader.rare_marks), which every write's claim sets
(check_input_rare_claim), and its vector path's hot reads give 0 where
no bit is set without touching the page (check_input_rare_written,
BUSTER_CHECK_RARE). The columns are base pages, which only their writes
fault in. The scalar path, the twin, reads every entry and counts the
bytes that a mark misses in CheckInput.visit_misses
(check_input_rare_verify); with a claim left out on purpose,
test-checker finds 212.

On the VM, -c sqlite3.c's peak falls from 255,160 to 249,208 KiB, for
893 more faults, with the objects the same bytes.

